k>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They mentioned that there are many microservices, and some or none might have faults. The initial attempt to get services from Grafana failed because of a name resolution error for jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local. Then, using kubectl to list services in the astronomy-shop namespace showed that jaeger is present as an ExternalName pointing to otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local. \n\nHmm, the next step is to figure out why the initial get_services call failed. The error was about not resolving jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local. But in the kubectl output, there\'s a jaeger service with type ExternalName pointing to otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local. Maybe the Grafana instance is trying to connect to jaeger-out, which doesn\'t exist, but the correct service is jaeger. So perhaps there\'s a typo or misconfiguration in the Grafana setup.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the jaeger service isn\'t properly set up. But according to the kubectl output, jaeger is present. Wait, the jaeger service is an ExternalName, which means it\'s just an alias. So if Grafana is configured to use jaeger-out, but that service doesn\'t exist, that would cause the resolution error. So the root cause might be a misconfigured service name in Grafana\'s setup, pointing to a non-existent jaeger-out service instead of the correct jaeger service.\n\nBut the user\'s main goal is to check for faults in the microservices. Since the initial get_services failed, maybe I should check the status of the jaeger service and related components. Let me check the jaeger service\'s endpoints. Wait, the jaeger service is an ExternalName, so it\'s not a real service but an alias. The actual service might be otel-collector. Maybe the otel-collector is not running or has issues. Let me check the status of the otel-collector pod.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is with the OpenTelemetry collector itself. Let me check the pods in the namespace to see if otel-collector is running. Using kubectl get pods in the astronomy-shop namespace would show the status of all pods. If otel-collector is not running, that could explain why Jaeger can\'t connect. Also, checking the logs of the jaeger services might help. But since the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, I can use that to get pod statuses and logs.\n\nWait, the user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to run commands like kubectl get pods. So first, I should check the status of all pods in the namespace to see if any are in a CrashLoopBackOff or Error state. That would indicate a problem with the services. Also, checking the logs of the jaeger-related pods might show why the connection is failing. But since the tool is read-only, I can\'t edit anything, but I can get logs.\n\nSo the next step is to run kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop to check the pod statuses. If any pods are not running, that\'s a problem. Then, check the logs of the jaeger or otel-collector pods to see if there are any errors. Alternatively, check the status of the otel-collector service to see if it\'s properly exposed.\n\nAnother angle: the initial error was about resolving jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local. But the kubectl output shows a jaeger service with ExternalName pointing to otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local. So maybe the Grafana instance is configured to use jaeger-out, which doesn\'t exist, leading to the resolution error. Therefore, the root cause could be a misconfiguration in Grafana\'s service name. However, the user\'s task is to check for faults in the microservices, so maybe the actual issue is with the jaeger service or the otel-collector.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the jaeger service is not properly set up. Let me check the jaeger service\'s YAML to see if it\'s correctly configured. But with the current tools, I can\'t do that. So using kubectl get svc -n astronomy-shop to check the jaeger service\'s details. Wait, the user already did kubectl get services, which showed jaeger as ExternalName. So the service is correctly defined as an alias. Therefore, the problem is likely that the Grafana instance is trying to connect to jaeger-out, which doesn\'t exist, leading to the resolution error. But the user\'s main task is to check for faults in the microservices, so maybe the jaeger service is not functioning properly, or the otel-collector is not running.\n\nSo the next step is to check the status of the otel-collector pod. Let me run kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop to see if the otel-collector pod is running. If it\'s not, that\'s a problem. If it is, then check the logs for any errors. Alternatively, check the status of the jaeger service\'s endpoints. But since jaeger is an ExternalName, it\'s pointing to otel-collector. So if otel-collector is not running, jaeger can\'t resolve.\n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to check their statuses. That would help identify if any critical services are not running, which could be the root cause.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo determine if any microservices are faulty, I need to check the current status of all pods in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. The `kubectl get pods` command will reveal if any pods are in a non-healthy state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error). This will help identify if any critical services are not running or experiencing issues, which could explain the earlier Jaeger resolution error or other faults. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=1252, prompt_tokens=3132, total_tokens=4384, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 's