</tool_call> - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0f47cad1-56ef-4d2b-894e-54201383c09e'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\n\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nastronomy-shop\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with an issue in their microservices application called the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop. They've encountered problems with Jaeger services not resolving, leading to errors in retrieving service lists and dependency graphs.\n\nFirst, they tried using the `get_services` tool, which failed because it couldn't resolve `jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local`. Then, when they checked the services with `kubectl get services`, they saw that there's a `jaeger` service, but it's an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. The `jaeger-out` service isn't listed. \n\nNext, they checked the pods and found all services are running. But when they tried `get_dependency_graph`, it failed for the same DNS resolution error. Then, describing the `jaeger` service showed it's correctly configured as an ExternalName. However, the application might be trying to connect to `jaeger-out` instead of `jaeger`, which doesn't exist. \n\nSo the root cause is that the `jaeger-out` service is missing. The `jaeger` service is correctly set up, but the application or other components are using the wrong service name. This leads to the NameResolutionError, causing issues with tracing and monitoring. The user needs to check if `jaeger-out` is supposed to be there and if there's a misconfiguration in the service references. Since the `jaeger` service is correctly configured, the problem is the absence of `jaeger-out`, which is likely a typo or a missing deployment.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is the absence of the `jaeger-out` service in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. The `jaeger` service exists but is misreferenced as `jaeger-out` in the application's configuration, leading to a **NameResolutionError**. This prevents Jaeger from functioning correctly, disrupting distributed tracing and monitoring for the microservices. \n\n**Fault Location:**  \n- Missing `jaeger-out` service in the `astronomy-shop` namespace.  \n- Misconfiguration in the application or components referencing `jaeger-out` instead of the correct `jaeger` service.  \n\n**Cause:**  \nThe application or monitoring tools attempt to resolve `jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local`, which does not exist. While the `jaeger` service is correctly configured as an `ExternalName` pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`, the absence of `jaeger-out` results in unresolved DNS errors, halting tracing and monitoring operations.  \n\n**Resolution:**  \nEnsure the `jaeger-out` service is properly deployed or update references in the application to use the correct `jaeger` service name. Verify service configurations and DNS entries to align with the actual deployed services.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7a1d911a-5b75-4b9a-9316-1c2d5b438901'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f51c5669-769d-4075-a4cd-161d13b5ecce'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop is having issues with Jaeger services not resolving. Specifically, there's a NameResolutionError when trying to access `jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local`. \n\nFirst, I need to verify the current state of the services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. The user tried using `get_services` and `kubectl get services`, which showed that the `jaeger` service exists but is an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. However, `jaeger-out` isn't listed. \n\nThe next step is to check if the `jaeger-out` service is missing. Since the application is trying to connect to `jaeger-out` but it's not present, that's the root cause. The `jaeger` service is correctly configured, but the application might be referencing the wrong service name. \n\nI should use the `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` tool to check the services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This will confirm if `jaeger-out` is missing. The command would be `kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop`. If `jaeger-out` isn't there, then the next step is to either deploy it or adjust the application's configuration to use the correct service name. \n\nAlternatively, if `jaeger-out` is supposed to be a different service, maybe there's a typo or a misconfiguration. But given the error message, it's clear that the service isn't present. So the immediate action is to verify the services and then address the missing service or the incorrect reference.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to check the current state of services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace, confirming whether `jaeger-out` exists or if there's a misconfiguration.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe error indicates `jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local` is unresolved. By running `kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop`, we can verify if the `jaeger-out` service is missing or misconfigured. This will clarify whether the fault lies in the absence of the service or an incorrect reference in the application's configuration.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nCheck the list of services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace to confirm the presence of `jaeger-out`.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 501, 'prompt_tokens': 2260, 'total_tokens': 2761, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdad-a72a-7d52-b37b-c230065819d3-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2260, 'output_tokens': 501, 'total_tokens': 2761}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='60a47c1c-04c7-47d6-be12-c3268ed6d6f1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why there\'s a NameResolutionError related to jaeger-out in their Kubernetes cluster. They mentioned that the get_services tool failed because it couldn\'t resolve jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local. Then, when they checked with kubectl get services in the astronomy-shop namespace, they found that the jaeger service exists but is an ExternalName pointing to otel-collector. However, jaeger-out isn\'t listed.\n\nSo the next step is to use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to run a command that lists all services in the astronomy-shop namespace. That way, they can confirm if jaeger-out is missing. The command should be "kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop". This will show the current services, helping to determine if jaeger-out is indeed absent or if there\'s a typo. If jaeger-out isn\'t there, the user might need to create it or adjust the application\'s configuration to use the correct service name. Alternatively, if there\'s a typo, they can correct it. The output of this command will provide clarity on the next steps.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8f749d68013f3b17', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 266, 'prompt_tokens': 3442, 'total_tokens': 3708, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool