quires a service name. If they try to get traces for the jaeger service, maybe that would show if there\'s an issue.\n\nWait, the user already tried get_traces and got an error because the service name was incorrect. So maybe the correct step is to check the ConfigMaps or Secrets in the namespace to find where the Jaeger service name is configured. If they can find the correct service name in the ConfigMap, they can update the application to use jaeger instead of jaeger-out.\n\nAlternatively, if the application is using a ConfigMap that\'s not in the namespace, they might need to check that. But since the user can\'t access the application\'s code, they have to rely on Kubernetes resources. So, checking ConfigMaps and Secrets in the astronomy-shop namespace for any entries related to Jaeger or tracing would be the next step.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to check ConfigMaps in the `astronomy-shop` namespace that might store Jaeger-related configuration (e.g., service names, endpoints). This will help identify if `jaeger-out` is hardcoded or misconfigured in the application\'s settings.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe absence of `jaeger-out` as a service or deployment suggests the application might be referencing it via ConfigMaps or Secrets. By inspecting ConfigMaps, we can verify if the service name is incorrectly set to `jaeger-out` instead of the correct `jaeger` service. This will confirm whether the fault lies in misconfigured application settings rather than missing infrastructure.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nCheck ConfigMaps in the `astronomy-shop` namespace for Jaeger-related configurations.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmaps -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 654, 'prompt_tokens': 4866, 'total_tokens': 5520, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdae-2e0a-7ed0-a83f-d56eb1f7f85b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4866, 'output_tokens': 654, 'total_tokens': 5520}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6fea0899-b1d2-42d9-9445-5b080c0b20d1'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with an issue in their microservices application called the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop. They've encountered problems with Jaeger services not resolving, leading to errors in retrieving service lists and dependency graphs.\n\nFirst, they tried using the `get_services` tool, which failed because it couldn't resolve `jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local`. Then, when they checked the services with `kubectl get services`, they saw that there's a `jaeger` service, but it's an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. The `jaeger-out` service isn't listed. \n\nNext, they checked the pods and found all services are running. But when they tried `get_dependency_graph`, it failed for the same DNS resolution error. Then, describing the `jaeger` service showed it's correctly configured as an ExternalName. However, the application might be trying to connect to `jaeger-out` instead of `jaeger`, which doesn't exist. \n\nSo the root cause is that the `jaeger-out` service is missing. The `jaeger` service is correctly set up, but the application or other components are using the wrong service name. This leads to the NameResolutionError, causing issues with tracing and monitoring. The user needs to check if `jaeger-out` is supposed to be there and if there's a misconfiguration in the service references. Since the `jaeger` service is correctly configured, the problem is the absence of `jaeger-out`, which is likely a typo or a missing deployment.\n\nThe user then tried checking deployments and pods, but `jaeger-out` wasn't found. They moved on to checking ConfigMaps to see if the service name is misconfigured there. The next step is to inspect the ConfigMaps for any references to `jaeger-out` that might be causing the application to use the wrong service name. If they find such a reference, they can correct it to use `jaeger` instead. If not, they might need to deploy the missing `jaeger-out` service or adjust the application's configuration accordingly.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmaps -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90339a41da00bcac', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 480, 'prompt_tokens': 6099, 'total_tokens': 6579, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdae-4d20-7ed1-b92c-1b1f44c13a57-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmaps -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90339a41da00bcac', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6099, 'output_tokens': 480, 'total_tokens': 6579}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                        DATA   AGE\nflagd-config                                1      5m40s\ngrafana                                     2      5m40s\ngrafana-alerting                            0      5m40s\ngrafana-config-dashboards                   1      5m40s\ngrafana-dashboard-apm-dashboard             1      5m40s\ngrafana-dashboard-demo-dashboard       