 deployment\'s current environment variables again to confirm the change. If it\'s still not updated, then maybe the command wasn\'t executed correctly, or there\'s a different issue. \n\nBut the user has already tried several methods. Since the last attempt with kubectl set env didn\'t work, perhaps they need to wait for the deployment to rollout and then check again. Alternatively, they could use kubectl rollout restart to force a restart of the deployment, which might apply the new configuration. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was to set the environment variable, and the response still shows the old value. That means the command didn\'t take effect. So the next step should be to verify the deployment\'s current state again, then possibly force a rollout or check for any errors in the command execution. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should check the logs of the frontend pod to see if there are any errors related to the trace endpoint. If the endpoint is incorrect, the application might be failing to connect, which could be logged. \n\nBut given the tools available, the user can only use kubectl commands. So after setting the environment variable, they should check the deployment\'s spec again. If it\'s still not updated, they might need to use kubectl rollout restart to trigger a new deployment. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to check the environment variable again to confirm the change, then proceed with a rollout if necessary.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name==\\"PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\\")].value}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8712fb4f8391d58c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 827, 'prompt_tokens': 9856, 'total_tokens'