        value: http://$(OTEL_COLLECTOR_NAME):4317\n        - name: WEB_OTEL_SERVICE_NAME\n          value: frontend-web\n        - name: PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\n          value: http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces\n        image: ghcr.io/open-telemetry/demo:2.2.0-frontend\n        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent\n        name: frontend\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 8080\n          name: service\n          protocol: TCP\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: 250Mi\n        securityContext:\n          runAsGroup: 1001\n          runAsNonRoot: true\n          runAsUser: 1001\n        terminationMessagePath: /dev/termination-log\n        terminationMessagePolicy: File\n      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst\n      restartPolicy: Always\n      schedulerName: default-scheduler\n      securityContext: {}\n      serviceAccount: astronomy-shop\n      serviceAccountName: astronomy-shop\n      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30\nstatus:\n  availableReplicas: 1\n  conditions:\n  - lastTransitionTime: "2026-03-17T21:18:15Z"\n    lastUpdateTime: "2026-03-17T21:18:15Z"\n    message: Deployment has minimum availability.\n    reason: MinimumReplicasAvailable\n    status: "True"\n    type: Available\n  - lastTransitionTime: "2026-03-17T21:17:36Z"\n    lastUpdateTime: "2026-03-17T21:18:15Z"\n    message: ReplicaSet "frontend-65c7cd5577" has successfully progressed.\n    reason: NewReplicaSetAvailable\n    status: "True"\n    type: Progressing\n  observedGeneration: 1\n  readyReplicas: 1\n  replicas: 1\n  updatedReplicas: 1\n', id='ba55e223-a6ab-4afe-a4ea-243da71fb85c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b07c9174519a5c20'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5a0c2918-2270-4a1f-a662-36b2858603cf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the issue where the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop is having problems with Jaeger services not resolving. The main error is a NameResolutionError for jaeger-out.observe.svc.cluster.local. \n\nFirst, I checked the services in the namespace and found that jaeger-out isn\'t listed. The jaeger service exists but is an ExternalName pointing to otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local. Then, looking at the pods, all services are running, but the application is trying to connect to jaeger-out, which doesn\'t exist.\n\nNext, I checked the frontend pod\'s environment variables and saw that the PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT is set to http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces. That\'s incorrect because it\'s using localhost instead of the correct service name. The Deployment YAML confirms this configuration.\n\nSo the root cause is that the frontend service is misconfigured to use localhost for the trace endpoint instead of the jaeger service. The solution is to update the PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT in the frontend Deployment to point to the correct jaeger service, which is jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local or the ExternalName otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local.\n\nNow, I need to modify the Deployment\'s environment variable to fix this. Using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool, I can edit the Deployment\'s YAML to correct the endpoint. After making the change, I should apply the updated configuration and verify that the service can resolve jaeger correctly. Once that\'s done, the tracing should work as expected, and the faults should be mitigated.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to update the `frontend` Deployment\'s environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` to use the correct Jaeger service endpoint (`jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local` instead of `localhost`). This will fix the misconfiguration causing the NameResolutionError.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe `frontend` Deployment\'s `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` is incorrectly set to `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`, which resolves to the pod\'s own IP and fails to connect to Jaeger. The correct endpoint should reference the `jaeger` service (or its ExternalName `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`). Updating this environment variable will ensure the application sends traces to the correct Jaeger service.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nEdit the `frontend` Deployment\'s YAML to fix the trace endpoint configuration.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: frontend\\n  namespace: astronomy-shop\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: frontend\\n        env:\\n        - name: PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\\n          value: http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces\\nEOF"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 674, 'prompt_tokens': 7728, 'total_tokens': 8402, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-2642-75c0-9ec2-4d166e479265-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7728, 'output_tokens': 674, 'total_tokens': 8402}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='00118548-3b2e-49d2-9b6e-28a00beab61b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me go through the steps again to make sure everything is correct. The user had an issue where the Jaeger service wasn't resolving, leading to errors. They checked the services and found that `jaeger-out` wasn't present, but `jaeger` was there as an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`.\n\nThe problem was in the `frontend` Deployment's environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT`, which was set to `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`. This was causing the application to try to connect to localhost instead of the correct Jaeger service. \n\nSo, the solution was to update that environment variable to use `jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local` instead. The user applied the change using `kubectl apply` with a YAML patch. Now, they need to verify if the change was successful. \n\nThe next step is to check the updated Deployment to ensure the environment variable was correctly modified. Then, they should confirm that the service is now resolving properly and that traces are being sent to Jaeger. If everything is fixed, they can submit the result. If not, they might need to check the service configuration again or look into other potential misconfigurations.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name==\\"PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\\")].value}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9ba17a9917f2d5fe', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 334, 'prompt_tokens': 9077, 'total_tokens': 9411, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-4743-77e3-8ee1-eaf44d3bf81c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\''}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9ba17a9917f2d5fe', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9077, 'output_tokens': 334, 'total_tokens': 9411}), ToolMessage(content='http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces', id='1a2caa76-117a-41ef-bd8d-c4379d6cd10f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9ba17a9917f2d5fe'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5b467b61-cc87-4e67-8bd5-99086a4cfcf3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue with their OpenTelemetry setup in the Astronomy Shop application. The problem seems to be that the Jaeger service isn\'t resolving correctly, leading to errors in tracing.\n\nFirst, they checked the services in the namespace and found that `jaeger-out` isn\'t listed, but `jaeger` exists as an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. Then, they looked into the frontend pod\'s environment variables and saw that the trace endpoint was set to `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`, which is incorrect. \n\nThey tried updating the Deployment to use `jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local` instead, but when they checked the environment variable again, it still showed the old value. That means the update didn\'t take effect. Maybe the kubectl apply command wasn\'t correctly formatted or there was an error in the YAML. \n\nWait, the user used a `kubectl apply -f -` with a YAML block. Maybe the command wasn\'t properly executed, or the YAML had a syntax error. Alternatively, the Deployment might not have been updated yet, so they need to wait for it to propagate. \n\nThey should verify if the Deployment was successfully updated. If the value is still `localhost`, then the apply command might have failed. They should check the Deployment\'s YAML again to see if the change was applied. If not, they might need to reapply the changes or check for any errors in the command. \n\nAlso, after updating the environment variable, they should wait a moment for the pods to restart and then check again. If the issue persists, maybe there\'s another misconfiguration or a different service that\'s causing the problem. But based on the current info, the main issue is the incorrect endpoint in the frontend\'s environment variables.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to force an update of the `frontend` Deployment and verify the corrected `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` value. If the previous `apply` command failed, this will ensure the change is applied and validated.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe previous `kubectl apply` attempt did not update the environment variable, as the value remained `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`. This suggests the YAML patch might not have been applied correctly. By explicitly reapplying the updated configuration and verifying the value, we ensure the fix is implemented.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nReapply the corrected configuration and validate the environment variable.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: frontend\\n  namespace: astronomy-shop\\nspec:\\n  template:\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: frontend\\n        env:\\n        - name: PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\\n          value: http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces\\nEOF"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 673, 'prompt_tokens': 8196, 'total_tokens': 8869, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-5b0a-7552-86fa-70dc1bd26ccc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8196, 'output_tokens': 673, 'total_tokens': 8869}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='83222dd8-5f7a-4480-abac-543350a72a64'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue where the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application isn't correctly sending traces to Jaeger. The problem seems to be that the frontend service is configured to use `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces` instead of the correct Jaeger service endpoint.\n\nFirst, they checked the pods and deployments, confirming that the `jaeger` service exists as an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. However, the frontend's environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` was still set to `localhost`, which isn't resolving. \n\nThey tried applying a patch to change the endpoint to `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces`, but when they checked the deployment again, the value hadn't changed. That suggests the apply command might not have worked as expected. \n\nNow, the user is reapplying the same configuration. The next step should be to verify if the change was successfully applied. If the value is still `localhost`, then there might be an issue with how the Kubernetes API is processing the update. Alternatively, maybe the deployment isn't rolling out correctly. \n\nTo ensure the change takes effect, they might need to force a rollout or check the deployment's status. But since the user has limited tool calls left, the best approach is to reapply the configuration and then check the environment variable again. If it's still not updated, they might need to consider other factors like service discovery or DNS issues. However, given the tools available