o directly update the `frontend` Deployment\'s environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` using `kubectl set env`, ensuring the correct Jaeger endpoint is applied.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe previous `kubectl apply` approach failed to update the environment variable, likely due to incorrect YAML formatting or patch application. Using `kubectl set env` directly targets the environment variable update, ensuring the correct value is applied. This is a more reliable method for modifying specific environment variables in a Deployment.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nUpdate the `frontend` Deployment\'s environment variable to use the correct Jaeger endpoint.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl set env deployment/frontend --namespace=astronomy-shop PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 703, 'prompt_tokens': 8620, 'total_tokens': 9323, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-936c-7543-9ce5-72fbc68d5964-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8620, 'output_tokens': 703, 'total_tokens': 9323}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bd8ee3c2-9289-4062-9529-a29383bb9cc5'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue where the OpenTelemetry traces aren\'t working in their Kubernetes cluster. The problem seems to be that the frontend service is pointing to the wrong endpoint for Jaeger. \n\nFirst, they checked the services and found that