serve.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces`. \n\nAlternatively, the `otel-collector` might be exposing the traces endpoint on a different path. The user should check the `otel-collector` service\'s configuration to confirm the correct endpoint. \n\nBut given the time constraints, the best approach is to update the environment variable to use the correct service name and port. So the correct value is `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces`. \n\nThe user should try applying this change again using `kubectl set env` or update the Deployment YAML. If that doesn\'t work, maybe there\'s a different issue, like the `otel-collector` not being configured to accept traces, or the service not being accessible. \n\nIn the next step, the user should attempt to set the environment variable to the correct value and verify it. If that fails, they might need to check the `otel-collector` configuration or ensure that the service is properly exposed.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to directly update the `frontend` Deployment\'s environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` to the correct value using `kubectl set env`, ensuring the change is applied.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe previous attempts to update the environment variable using `kubectl apply` and `kubectl set env` failed to change the value, likely due to incorrect formatting or caching. Using `kubectl set env` directly ensures the environment variable is updated correctly. If this still fails, the issue may lie in the `otel-collector` configuration or service resolution.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nForce update the `frontend` Deployment\'s environment variable to use the correct Jaeger endpoint.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl set env deployment/frontend --namespace=astronomy-shop PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1588, 'prompt_tokens': 8975, 'total_tokens': 10563, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-e183-7510-9f29-b3b68389c8bd-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8975, 'output_tokens': 1588, 'total_tokens': 10563}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d75ca1cd-c43f-412d-88cd-036bf1262bb8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue where the OpenTelemetry traces aren\'t being sent to Jaeger correctly in their Kubernetes cluster. The problem seems to be with the environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` in the `frontend` Deployment. \n\nFirst, they checked the current value of this variable using `kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\'` and found it\'s still set to `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`. That\'s incorrect because the correct endpoint should point to the Jaeger service, which is `jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local` or `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local` via the `jaeger` ExternalName.\n\nThey tried applying a YAML patch and using `kubectl set env` multiple times, but the value didn\'t change. The user might be facing an issue where the Deployment isn\'t updating correctly, possibly due to a typo in the service name or a misconfiguration in the `otel-collector` service. \n\nLooking at the `jaeger` service details, it\'s an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. So the correct endpoint should be `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces` or `http://otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces`. \n\nThe user\'s last attempt used `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces`, which might be incorrect because the port for OTLP traces is usually 4317, but the path might need to be `/v1/traces` instead of `/otlp-http/v1/traces`. Wait, the correct OTLP endpoint path is typically `/v1/traces`, so maybe the user should use `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local:4317/v1/traces`.\n\nBut the previous attempts didn\'t work, so maybe the issue is that the `jaeger` service isn\'t properly configured or there\'s a network policy blocking access. Alternatively, the `otel-collector` might not be set up to accept traces from the frontend. \n\nAnother angle: the `frontend` pod\'s environment variable is set to `localhost`, which is the pod\'s own IP. That\'s why it\'s not reaching Jaeger. The correct approach is to ensure the endpoint points to the Jaeger service within the cluster. \n\nSince the user tried `kubectl set env` and it didn\'t work, maybe they need to check if the Deployment is rolling out the changes. They could use `kubectl rollout status deployment/frontend -n astronomy-shop` to see if the update is progressing. If it\'s stuck, there might be a problem with the Deployment configuration or resource limits.\n\nAlternatively, the user could check the logs of the `frontend` pod to see if there are any errors related to the trace endpoint. Using `kubectl logs frontend-65c7cd5577-vstck -n astronomy-shop` might reveal if the application is unable to connect to the specified endpoint.\n\nAnother possibility is that the `jaeger` service isn\'t properly exposed. The user should verify the `jaeger` service\'s configuration with `kubectl get service jaeger -n astronomy-shop` to ensure it\'s an ExternalName pointing to the correct `otel-collector` service. If the service 