us errors, the user is trying to use JSONPath directly. The error message mentioned "unclosed array expect ]", which suggests that the JSONPath expression isn\'t properly closed. Let me check the command again. The user\'s command ends with `]`.value\'`, which might be missing a closing bracket. Wait, the JSONPath structure is:  \n`.items[?(...)].spec.containers[0].env[?(...)].value`\n\nSo after the `items` array, there\'s a `]` to close it, then `.spec...` etc. The user\'s command seems to have that. Maybe the issue is with the quotes. Using double quotes inside the JSONPath might be causing problems. Let me try using single quotes for the entire JSONPath expression and double quotes inside, or escape the double quotes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the `opentelemetry.io/name` label is not correctly formatted. The correct label selector should be `opentelemetry.io/name=frontend`, but in JSONPath, it\'s written as `@.metadata.labels."opentelemetry.io/name" == "frontend"`. Wait, in JSONPath, the syntax for selecting a label is `@.metadata.labels[\'opentelemetry.io/name\'] == \'frontend\'`, but using double quotes might require escaping. Alternatively, using single quotes for the label key.\n\nAnother possibility is that the `items` array is not properly closed. Let me re-express the JSONPath:\n\n`{.items[?(@.metadata.labels."opentelemetry.io/name"=="frontend")].spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}`\n\nWait, after the `items` array, there\'s a `]` to close it, then `.spec...`. The user\'s command seems to have that. Maybe the issue is with the way the quotes are handled. Let me try using single quotes for the entire JSONPath and double quotes inside, or escape the double quotes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the `env` array is not properly accessed. The correct path would be `.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value`. So the JSONPath should be correctly structured.\n\nGiven the previous error, the user might need to adjust the JSONPath syntax to avoid the unclosed array. Perhaps the correct command is:\n\n`kubectl get pod -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.items[?(@.metadata.labels."opentelemetry.io/name"=="frontend")].spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\'`\n\nBut the error persists. Maybe the issue is that the `items` array is not properly closed, or the label selector is incorrect. Alternatively, the pod might not have the label `opentelemetry.io/name` set, or the value is different. Let me check the pod\'s labels again.\n\nEarlier, when the user ran `kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop`, the pod `frontend-65c7cd5577-vstck` had labels including `opentelemetry.io/name: frontend`. So the label selector should work.\n\nAnother approach: Maybe the user should first get the pod\'s name using `kubectl get pods -l "opentelemetry.io/name=frontend" -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].metadata.name}\'`, then use that name to get the environment variable. But the user is trying to do it in one command.\n\nAlternatively, using `kubectl get pod -n astronomy-shop -l "opentelemetry.io/name=frontend" -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\'` might be simpler, as it directly filters the pod by label and accesses the environment variable.\n\nBut the user\'s previous attempt had a syntax error. Let me adjust the JSONPath to use single quotes for the label selector and ensure proper syntax. The correct command should be:\n\n`kubectl get pod -n astronomy-shop -l "opentelemetry.io/name=frontend" -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\'`\n\nThis way, the pod is filtered by label, and the environment variable is retrieved directly. This avoids the need for the `items` array and simplifies the JSONPath. This might resolve the syntax error and successfully retrieve the value.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'