quires a service name. If they try to get traces for the jaeger service, maybe that would show if there\'s an issue.\n\nWait, the user already tried get_traces and got an error because the service name was incorrect. So maybe the correct step is to check the ConfigMaps or Secrets in the namespace to find where the Jaeger service name is configured. If they can find the correct service name in the ConfigMap, they can update the application to use jaeger instead of jaeger-out.\n\nAlternatively, if the application is using a ConfigMap that\'s not in the namespace, they might need to check that. But since the user can\'t access the application\'s code, they have to rely on Kubernetes resources. So, checking ConfigMaps and Secrets in the astronomy-shop namespace for any entries related to Jaeger or tracing would be the next step.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \ntool description: This tool will execute a `kubectl` command to check ConfigMaps in the `astronomy-shop` namespace that might store Jaeger-related configuration (e.g., service names, endpoints). This will help identify if `jaeger-out` is hardcoded or misconfigured in the application\'s settings.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nThe absence of `jaeger-out` as a service or deployment suggests the application might be referencing it via ConfigMaps or Secrets. By inspecting ConfigMaps, we can verify if the service name is incorrectly set to `jaeger-out` instead of the correct `jaeger` service. This will confirm whether the fault lies in misconfigured application settings rather than missing infrastructure.  \n\n**Next step:**  \nCheck ConfigMaps in the `astronomy-shop` namespace for Jaeger-related configurations.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmaps -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 654, 'prompt_tokens