, reapplying the patch and verifying is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name==\\"PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT\\")].value}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9518512334321ad3', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 425, 'prompt_tokens': 9501, 'total_tokens': 9926, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdaf-7bbc-7170-86f8-0b89a9cc8052-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name=="PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT")].value}\''}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9518512334321ad3', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9501, 'output_tokens': 425, 'total_tokens': 9926}), ToolMessage(content='http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces', id='98d0cb69-8aed-49f9-b148-73eef1fda1b3', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9518512334321ad3'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4b95d533-2ca2-43ac-9f0e-2f790705f423'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the issue where the OpenTelemetry traces aren\'t being sent to Jaeger. The problem seems to be that the frontend service is configured to use `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces` instead of the correct Jaeger service endpoint.\n\nFirst, I checked the deployment YAML for the frontend and saw that the environment variable `PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` was set to localhost. I tried applying a fix to change it to `http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces`, but the value didn\'t update. The tool response still shows the old value, so the change wasn\'t applied correctly.\n\nHmm, maybe the way I applied the patch was wrong. I should try a different approach. Instead of using `kubectl apply` with a YAML patch, perhaps I should use `kubectl set env` to directly update the environment variable. That command is more straightforward for modifying environment variables in a deployment.\n\nLet me check the syntax for `kubectl set env`. The correct command would be `kubectl set env deployment/frontend --namespace=astronomy-shop PUBLIC_OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local/otlp-http/v1/traces`. This should update the deployment\'s environment variable without needing to rewrite the entire YAML.\n\nAfter applying this command, I need to verify that the change took effect. I\'ll use the same JSONPath command to check the environment variable again. If it\'s updated, the issue is resolved. If not, maybe there\'s a problem with the deployment\'s rollout, and I might need to force a restart or check for errors in the deployment logs.\n\nWait, the user might also need to ensure that the Jaeger service is correctly configured. Earlier, the `jaeger` service was an ExternalName pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`, which should be correct. But if the frontend is still using localhost, the traces won\'t reach Jaeger. So the main issue is the environment variable in the frontend deployment.\n\nI should proceed with the `kubectl set env` command and then verify the change. If that works, the traces should start flowing to Jaeger. If not, I\'ll need to investigate further, maybe check the deployment\'s rollout status or look into any potential conflicts.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  