LP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` in the `frontend` Deployment. \n\nFirst, they checked the services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace and found that `jaeger` is an `ExternalName` pointing to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`. But the application was trying to connect to `jaeger-out`, which doesn't exist. Then, they looked into the `frontend` pod's environment variables and saw that the trace endpoint was set to `http://localhost:8080/otlp-http/v1/traces`, which is incorrect.\n\nThey tried updating the endpoint to `jaeger.observe.svc.cluster.local` and later to `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local`, but the changes weren't taking effect. The deployment rolled out successfully, but the environment variable still showed the old value. This suggests that maybe the changes weren't applied correctly, or there's a caching issue. \n\nThe user might need to force a restart of the pod to apply the new environment variable. Alternatively, there could be a misconfiguration in the service name. Since the `jaeger` service is an `ExternalName` pointing to `otel-collector`, using `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local` should work. But if the application is still not using it, maybe the environment variable isn't being updated properly. \n\nAnother possibility is that the `otel-collector` service isn't running or isn't accessible. They should check the status of the `otel-collector` service and ensure it's up and running. If it's down, that would explain why the traces aren't being sent. \n\nAlternatively, there might be a typo in the service name. The user should verify that `otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local` is the correct DNS name for the `otel-collector` service. If there's a typo, the application would fail to connect. \n\nSince the previous attempts to update the environment variable didn't work, the next step should be to check the current value again to confirm if it's been updated. If it's still incorrect, they might need to use a different method to apply the 